      { id: "qna-case-noisy-training-01", level: 3, q: "Your team is training a continuous-control policy with plain REINFORCE, and episode-to-episode returns look almost random even though the policy doesn't seem to have plateaued. Walk through your diagnosis and the levers you'd pull, in order, before concluding the algorithm itself is wrong for the task.", difficulty: "hard" },
      { id: "qna-case-value-to-pg-switch-01", level: 3, q: "A colleague wants to switch a project from a value-based method to a policy gradient method purely because the action space just changed from a small discrete set to a continuous range. Walk me through whether that's the right call, and what specifically breaks in the value-based approach that justifies it.", difficulty: "medium" },
      { id: "qna-case-missing-baseline-01", level: 3, q: "You inherit a REINFORCE implementation and notice the update uses the raw return G_t with no baseline subtracted anywhere. What would you expect to see in the training curves as a symptom of this, and what's the smallest change you'd make to fix it?", difficulty: "medium" },
      { id: "qna-case-stochastic-necessity-01", level: 3, q: "Someone asks why you can't just use a deterministic policy in a competitive, adversarial two-player setting instead of a stochastic one. Using only the reasoning about policy representation from this module, explain why that's a real constraint rather than just a design preference.", difficulty: "medium" }
